1
00:00:00,449 --> 00:00:02,734
An AI agent calls a payment tool.

2
00:00:02,734 --> 00:00:04,833
The tool replies: ok.

3
00:00:04,833 --> 00:00:06,402
Did the money actually move?

4
00:00:06,402 --> 00:00:08,772
Here is the catch. The same request can get the

5
00:00:08,772 --> 00:00:11,272
same reply in four different realities.

6
00:00:11,272 --> 00:00:13,760
Sometimes ok means nothing was paid at all.

7
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:16,319
Sometimes a timeout means you already paid, and

8
00:00:16,319 --> 00:00:18,901
a blind retry pays the supplier twice.

9
00:00:19,701 --> 00:00:22,434
We built ActionProof to measure exactly this.

10
00:00:22,434 --> 00:00:25,594
On 440 locked tasks, an agent that trusts the

11
00:00:25,594 --> 00:00:28,566
reply falsely reports success 86 percent of the

12
00:00:28,566 --> 00:00:31,266
time. Retrying with idempotency keys stops

13
00:00:31,266 --> 00:00:33,590
double payments, but it still reports phantom

14
00:00:33,590 --> 00:00:34,862
payments as done.

15
00:00:35,662 --> 00:00:37,938
So we trained a three billion parameter Cohere

16
00:00:37,938 --> 00:00:39,716
model to behave like an auditor.

17
00:00:39,716 --> 00:00:41,332
Here is its run on our Space:

18
00:00:41,332 --> 00:00:43,041
the labelled recording of the same greedy

19
00:00:43,041 --> 00:00:45,093
model, task and reality.

20
00:00:45,093 --> 00:00:46,508
The payment is a phantom:

21
00:00:46,508 --> 00:00:49,091
the tool says ok, but nothing is written.

22
00:00:49,091 --> 00:00:51,249
The reply-trusting script believes it, and

23
00:00:51,249 --> 00:00:53,761
reports done. Our agent does not.

24
00:00:53,761 --> 00:00:55,329
It reads the payment ledger.

25
00:00:55,329 --> 00:00:57,983
Empty. So it pays again, with the same

26
00:00:57,983 --> 00:01:00,648
idempotency key, so a real first payment could

27
00:01:00,648 --> 00:01:02,240
